37384033203125, 0.021453857421875, 0.01259613037109375, 0.01483917236328125, -0.0333251953125, 0.017822265625, 0.0070953369140625, -0.018280029296875, 0.0301055908203125, 0.0218963623046875, 0.019195556640625, -0.0085906982421875, -0.003631591796875, 0.01213836669921875, -0.004848480224609375, -0.02386474609375, -0.0215606689453125, -0.01386260986328125, -0.0249481201171875, -0.0491943359375, 0.0038471221923828125, -0.00241851806640625, -0.0085906982421875, 0.005657196044921875, -0.01554107666015625, 0.01386260986328125, -0.01849365234375, 0.033660888671875, 0.0195159912109375, 0.0190582275390625, 0.02081298828125, -0.0313720703125, 0.026519775390625, 0.040679931640625, 0.0239715576171875, 0.01230621337890625, 0.007747650146484375, -0.052886962890625, -0.0177154541015625, 0.003662109375, 0.004405975341796875, 0.01873779296875, 0.01012420654296875, 0.0010671615600585938, 0.00030803680419921875, -0.0254058837890625, 0.01849365234375, -0.0031833648681640625, -0.0209808349609375, -0.002651214599609375, 0.0037059783935546875, -0.0205078125, -0.0218963623046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Democracy can be defined in various ways, and debates over its meaning have significant implications for both scholarship and policy. This document examines the central question: Should democracy be defined narrowly by its procedures, or more broadly to include substantive rights and freedoms? Two prominent approaches\u2014minimalist and maximalist definitions\u2014illustrate this debate.\n\nA minimalist definition of democracy refers to a system in which the primary criterion is the existence of regular, competitive elections that allow citizens to choose their leaders. Joseph Schumpeter, in his influential work \"Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy\" (1942), is often associated with this perspective. He argued that democracy is best understood as an institutional arrangement for arriving at political decisions in which individuals acquire the power to decide by means of a competitive struggle for the people\u2019s vote. For example, contemporary Russia holds regular elections, but critics argue that these elections are neither free nor fair, raising questions about whether the minimalist criteria are truly met.\n\nIn contrast, a maximalist definition includes not only electoral procedures but also a broader set of requirements, such as the protection of civil liberties and human rights. Robert Dahl, particularly in \"Polyarchy\" (1971), advanced this view by emphasizing not only free and fair elections but also the necessity of freedoms like speech, assembly, and equal legal protection as essential components of a democratic system. Sweden, for instance, is often cited as a maximalist democracy, as it combines competitive elections with strong protections for civil liberties and minority rights.\n\nEach definition has attracted criticism from different scholars and schools of thought. The minimalist approach is often faulted, notably by theorists such as Larry Diamond and advocates of liberal democracy, for ignoring substantive elements of democracy, such as the actual protection of rights or the inclusiveness of participation. They argue that a country might hold regular elections but suppress opposition voices or restrict media freedom\u2014Turkey in recent years is an example\u2014thus meeting the minimalist criteria while failing to uphold what many consider core democratic values, such as pluralism and freedom of expression. The consequence is that minimalist definitions may legitimize regimes that are democratic in form but not in substance.\n\nConversely, maximalist definitions are sometimes criticized, for example by Adam Przeworski and proponents of procedural democracy, for being too broad or difficult to operationalize. Including a wide range of civil liberties and social rights can make it challenging to determine whether a country qualifies as a democracy, as the measurement of these rights can be subjective and vary across contexts. This can lead to disagreements over classification and hinder comparative research. For instance, the question of whether India, with its robust elections but contested civil liberties, qualifies as a democracy depends on the definition used.\n\nThe distinction between these definitions is not merely academic; it has practical consequences. International organizations such as Freedom House and the Economist Intelligence Unit use different criteria to rate countries, which can affect foreign aid decisions, diplomatic recognition, and the design of democracy promotion programs. For example, a country classified as a democracy under minimalist criteria might receive support or legitimacy that it would not under a maximalist assessment.\n\nTerms such as \"democratic values\" refer to principles like political equality, participation, and protection of individual rights, while \"democratic practices\" encompass both the procedures (e.g., elections) and the substantive freedoms (e.g., free press, rule of law) that characterize a functioning democracy. The \"quality and legitimacy of political systems\" thus depend on both how power is acquired and how it is exercised in relation to citizens' rights.\n\nIn conclusion, the complexity and contested nature of the concept of democracy stem from disagreements over which features are essential and how they should be measured. These definitional debates are significant for ongoing scholarship, as they shape research agendas and comparative analyses, and for policymaking, as they influence how democratic progress is assessed and promoted. Future research and policy should be explicit about the definitions employed, as this clarity is crucial for understanding the diversity of democratic practices worldwide and for making informed judgments about political systems."}}